# Straight-line block execution

`runXLocatedBlock path s` is rewritten to a left fold `path.foldl stepL (some s)`
(`runXLocatedBlock_eq_foldl`). Under `simp`, `List.foldl_cons` then exposes one
instruction at a time: the head `stepL (some st) l` is reduced to `some st'`
(via `stepL_located`, `runXInstr` and the simprocs of `Infra.Code`) before the
next fold step is visited. `Infra.Reflect` proves blocks by reflection; this
tactic is its fallback (a jump to a symbolic return address).

The tactic `glam44_block [extra lemmas]` packages the whole recipe. It runs the
path in pieces of 32 instructions (`foldl_chunks`, `MlDsaOptCommon.Chunk`):
one `simp` over a long path makes a proof whose kernel check grows much faster
than the path. The `halt = .Running` premise is closed by
`with_reducible assumption` or `simp` (plain `assumption` unfolded the state
against every hypothesis: about 30 s of `MlDsaOpt44.Fn.Fix.Blocks`). Side
goals of the `simp` go to `blk_disch` (`MlDsaOptCommon.Disch`).
-/

